TASK [forge-dns : Render this role's drop-ins] *********************************
--- before
+++ after: /home/developer/.ansible/tmp/ansible-local-582690o99ovvka/tmpsjlpfrub/05-forge.hosts.j2
@@ -0,0 +1,67 @@
+# Ansible managed
+# Managed by homelab-infra: ansible/roles/forge-dns
+#
+# Hosts-format sidecar. It is pulled in by the single `addn-hosts=` line in
+# 00-forge-hosts.conf — NOT by 05-forge.conf; see that
+# file's header for why the declaration lives in its own 00- prefixed drop-in.
+#
+# WHY HOSTS FORMAT: a hosts entry makes the NAME exist in dnsmasq's local
+# namespace, so A answers the address below and AAAA answers NOERROR/ANSWER: 0
+# (NODATA) instead of the NXDOMAIN that `local=/bi-services.be/` would
+# otherwise synthesise. NXDOMAIN is cached by Windows against the NAME, which
+# is what made Chrome report "server IP address could not be found".
+#
+# WHY A SUBDIRECTORY: `conf-dir=/etc/dnsmasq.d` on this image carries no
+# `,*.conf` filter, so a hosts file sitting directly in that directory is
+# parsed as config and kills pihole-FTL. conf-dir skips subdirectories.
+#
+# FQDNs ONLY. 02-homelab.conf sets `expand-hosts` with `domain=bi-services.be`,
+# so a single-label name here would silently mint <label>.bi-services.be as an
+# extra record. Every name below is already fully qualified.
+#
+# This file is NOT /etc/pihole/custom.list and must never be conflated with it:
+# custom.list is read-only, holds hand-added records (hermes.bi-services.be),
+# and is never read or written by this role.
+#
+# Editing only this file would take effect on SIGHUP, but the role re-execs
+# pihole-FTL for a change to any of its three files. Records are sourced from
+# Terraform (terraform/envs/homelab/outputs.tf); see roles/forge-dns/defaults
+# for the per-record provenance.
+#
+# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
+# REVERSE ZONE — what this file does and does not change
+# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
+# dnsmasq answers a PTR with the FIRST-loaded name for an address, and reads
+# addn-hosts files in reverse declaration order. Declaring this file from
+# 00-forge-hosts.conf (which conf-dir reads BEFORE 01-pihole.conf)
+# makes /etc/pihole/custom.list load first, so custom.list keeps every reverse
+# answer it has today. FTL log, in load order:
+#     read /etc/pihole/custom.list - 37 names
+#     read /etc/dnsmasq.d/forge/05-forge.hosts - 5 names
+#
+# Measured net effect against the unmodified production resolver (dig -x):
+#     192.168.50.194   alertmanager.bi-services.be -> alertmanager...   UNCHANGED
+#     192.168.50.167   NXDOMAIN                    -> forge...          added
+#     192.168.50.164   NXDOMAIN                    -> ct-saply-ai...    added
+#     192.168.50.165   NXDOMAIN                    -> ct-crypto-quant   added
+# The three additions are addresses that had no reverse name at all. Nothing in
+# homelab-infra or homelab-apps consumes a PTR (grepped for in-addr.arpa,
+# `dig -x`, and reverse-DNS relabeling in the Prometheus and log config — zero
+# hits), and Pi-hole runs no conditional forwarding and no rev-server.
+#
+# The earlier single-file layout (addn-hosts= declared from
+# 05-forge.conf) DID flip 192.168.50.194 to gemma.bi-services.be.
+# That is what the 00- split exists to avoid. Forward resolution was identical
+# either way, so if this role is ever collapsed back into one file, expect that
+# reverse answer to move again.
+#
+# IF THIS FILE GOES MISSING: `pihole-FTL dnsmasq-test` still passes, FTL starts,
+# and it logs `failed to load names from ...` — every other name keeps
+# resolving and only the 5 names below go
+# NXDOMAIN. The syntax gate therefore cannot catch it; the role's per-name dig
+# verification is what does.
+192.168.50.167 forge.bi-services.be
+192.168.50.194 gemma.bi-services.be
+192.168.50.167 ct-forge-01.bi-services.be
+192.168.50.164 ct-saply-ai.bi-services.be
+192.168.50.165 ct-crypto-quant.bi-services.be

changed: [ct-docker-01] => (item=/opt/appdata/dns-pihole/dnsmasq.d/forge/05-forge.hosts)
--- before
+++ after: /home/developer/.ansible/tmp/ansible-local-582690o99ovvka/tmp1kdbd3zy/00-forge-hosts.conf.j2
@@ -0,0 +1,56 @@
+# Ansible managed
+# Managed by homelab-infra: ansible/roles/forge-dns
+#
+# ONE LINE, IN ITS OWN FILE, WITH A 00- PREFIX — all three on purpose.
+#
+# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
+# WHY A SEPARATE DROP-IN: the reverse zone
+# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
+# dnsmasq holds addn-hosts targets in a LIFO list — the file declared LAST is
+# read FIRST — and answers a reverse (PTR) lookup with the FIRST-loaded name
+# for an address. /etc/pihole/custom.list is declared by Pi-hole's own
+# 01-pihole.conf, so where this role declares its hosts file decides whether
+# custom.list still wins PTR for the addresses both files name.
+#
+# Measured on pihole/pihole:2024.07.0 (pi-hole-v2.90+1 / FTL v5.25.2), same
+# harness, same records, only the declaring file moved:
+#
+#   declared from 05-forge.conf (sorts AFTER 01-pihole.conf):
+#     FTL log order:  read /etc/dnsmasq.d/forge/05-forge.hosts - 5 names
+#                     read /etc/pihole/custom.list - 37 names
+#     dig -x 192.168.50.194  ->  gemma.bi-services.be          CHANGED
+#
+#   declared from THIS file (sorts BEFORE 01-pihole.conf):
+#     FTL log order:  read /etc/pihole/custom.list - 37 names
+#                     read /etc/dnsmasq.d/forge/05-forge.hosts - 5 names
+#     dig -x 192.168.50.194  ->  alertmanager.bi-services.be   UNCHANGED
+#
+# Forward resolution is byte-for-byte identical between the two layouts: the
+# forge apex and wildcard labels, all five exact names, hermes, alertmanager,
+# grafana and the NXDOMAIN negative controls all answer the same either way.
+# So the split costs one extra file and buys back a reverse-zone answer that
+# is outside this fix's blast radius. .167/.164/.165 still GAIN a PTR where
+# they had none — those are pure additions, not changes.
+#
+# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
+# WHY THE TARGET IS IN A SUBDIRECTORY
+# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
+# /etc/dnsmasq.conf on this image is exactly
+# `conf-dir=/etc/dnsmasq.d` with NO `,*.conf` filter, so every regular file in
+# that directory is parsed as dnsmasq CONFIG. A hosts file placed there fails
+# with "dnsmasq: bad option at line 2" and pihole-FTL enters an s6
+# crash-restart loop — a full LAN DNS outage, measured. conf-dir skips
+# subdirectories, so forge/ is safe.
+#
+# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
+# IF THE TARGET IS MISSING
+# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
+# A dangling addn-hosts= is TOLERATED, not fatal (measured): `pihole-FTL
+# dnsmasq-test` still prints "dnsmasq: syntax check OK." and exits 0, FTL
+# starts normally and logs
+#   failed to load names from /etc/dnsmasq.d/forge/05-forge.hosts: No such file or directory
+# while every other name — hermes, alertmanager, the wildcard subtree — keeps
+# resolving. Only the exact names in that file go NXDOMAIN. That also means
+# the syntax gate in tasks/main.yml CANNOT catch a missing target; the role
+# renders the hosts file before this one and verifies each name by dig instead.
+addn-hosts=/etc/dnsmasq.d/forge/05-forge.hosts

changed: [ct-docker-01] => (item=/opt/appdata/dns-pihole/dnsmasq.d/00-forge-hosts.conf)
--- before: /opt/appdata/dns-pihole/dnsmasq.d/05-forge.conf
+++ after: /home/developer/.ansible/tmp/ansible-local-582690o99ovvka/tmpmqf42qyu/05-forge.conf.j2
@@ -2,22 +2,99 @@
 # Managed by homelab-infra: ansible/roles/forge-dns
 #
 # ADDITIVE internal DNS for the Forge (Coder) PoC on ct-forge-01 (CT 167).
+# This file carries the WILDCARD subtrees only; the exact Terraform names are
+# hosts-format records pulled in by 00-forge-hosts.conf.
 #
-# Landed as a dnsmasq drop-in rather than through /etc/pihole/custom.list on
-# purpose: custom.list is bind-mounted read-only from the homelab-apps clone and
-# contains hand-added records (notably `192.168.50.194 hermes.bi-services.be`,
-# the live Linear -> Hermes webhook router) that are NOT in Terraform's DNS map.
-# Rewriting custom.list from Terraform's generated copy would delete them.
-# This file adds records without reading or replacing any existing record.
+# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
+# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS AND NOT custom.list
+# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
+# /etc/pihole/custom.list is bind-mounted READ-ONLY from the homelab-apps clone
+# and carries hand-added records — notably `192.168.50.194 hermes.bi-services.be`,
+# the live Linear -> Hermes webhook router — that are NOT in Terraform's DNS
+# map. Landing Terraform's generated/pihole-custom.list over it would delete
+# them. Everything here is added; no existing record is read or replaced.
 #
 # Follow-up (tracked, NOT done here): reconcile hermes into Terraform's DNS map
 # and land generated/pihole-custom.list as additions-only.
 #
-# Changing this file only takes effect once pihole-FTL is restarted. Do NOT
-# run `pihole restartdns` on this image: it hangs forever and can leave the
-# s6 service marked down, taking homelab DNS with it. Re-run the role.
+# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
+# WHY NODATA, NOT NXDOMAIN — the Windows failure this fixes
+# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
+# `address=/<domain>/<ipv4>` matches A queries only. Any other qtype for the
+# subtree used to fall through to `local=/bi-services.be/` in 02-homelab.conf,
+# which synthesises NXDOMAIN. NXDOMAIN means "this NAME does not exist" — not
+# "this name has no AAAA" — and the Windows DNS client caches it against the
+# NAME. Chrome then reports "server IP address could not be found" for a name
+# whose A record is perfect, because Windows asks AAAA first and never retries.
 #
-# `address=/<domain>/<ip>` is a wildcard match: it answers <domain> and every
-# name under it. dnsmasq prefers the most specific match, so this takes
-# precedence over the `local=/bi-services.be/` catch-all in 02-homelab.conf.
+# The correct negative answer is NODATA: rcode NOERROR with ANSWER: 0. That is
+# what hermes.bi-services.be has always returned (it is a hosts entry, so the
+# name exists for every qtype), and it is the shape reproduced below.
+#
+# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
+# TWO MECHANISMS, ONE FOR EACH KIND OF NAME
+# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
+# 1. WILDCARD SUBTREES (this file). Coder's workspace preview URLs are
+#    subdomain-based (*.forge.bi-services.be), so an arbitrary label must
+#    resolve. A hosts file cannot express that — a hosts file has no wildcard,
+#    and ADR-005/FE2-S05-AC5 forbid an asterisk ever entering the generated
+#    hosts map. Hence the address= pair: the IPv4 form serves A for the apex
+#    and every label under it; the BARE form (no address after the final "/")
+#    makes every OTHER qtype answer NOERROR/ANSWER: 0 from config.
+#
+#    Measured on this exact image (pihole/pihole:2024.07.0, pi-hole-v2.90+1,
+#    FTL v5.25.2): the FTL query log shows `config <name> is NODATA-IPv6` and
+#    `config <name> is NODATA`, with no `forwarded ... to` line — the answer is
+#    local. Line order between the two forms does not matter. Re-measure this
+#    before any Pi-hole image bump: v6 drops dnsmasq config for pihole.toml.
+#    The bare form answers EVERY non-A qtype for the subtree — TXT, SRV and
+#    HTTPS/SVCB (type 65) included — NODATA from local config, so an internal
+#    DNS-01 challenge under forge. would need a narrower directive than this
+#    pair rather than a TXT record dnsmasq will never serve.
+#
+# 2. EXACT NAMES (05-forge.hosts, declared from 00-forge-hosts.conf).
+#    The five Terraform names are NOT under forge., so the wildcard cannot
+#    cover them, and an address= per name would reintroduce the AAAA-NXDOMAIN
+#    bug one name at a time. A hosts entry makes the NAME exist, so A answers
+#    the IP and AAAA answers NODATA for free — exactly how hermes behaves. A
+#    hosts entry also OVERRIDES address= for the exact name it declares
+#    (measured), while leaving the wildcard in force for every other label,
+#    which is why forge.bi-services.be safely appears in both places.
+#
+# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
+# WHY NOT server=/forge.bi-services.be/# (the forwarding alternative)
+# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
+# Forwarding the subtree to the public Cloudflare zone also produces NODATA for
+# AAAA, and was measured working. It was rejected: with the upstream
+# unreachable, AAAA for the subtree got NO ANSWER AT ALL and dig gave up after
+# 15.0 s, turning a fast wrong answer into a 15-second stall. It would also
+# make internal DNS correctness depend on a public zone continuing to publish
+# unproxied RFC1918 A records. Under an identical dead-upstream test, the
+# address= pair below answered every probe in ~18 ms: it is served from local
+# config and has ZERO upstream dependency. Losing 8.8.8.8/8.8.4.4 degrades
+# ordinary internet resolution only, exactly as it does today.
+#
+# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
+# RELOAD SEMANTICS
+# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
+# Changing THIS file takes effect only after a full pihole-FTL re-exec: dnsmasq
+# re-reads hosts files on SIGHUP but never re-reads its config files (measured
+# — a newly added address= still answered NXDOMAIN after SIGHUP). Changing only
+# 05-forge.hosts would be a SIGHUP away, and there is no inotify
+# auto-reload for addn-hosts. The role nevertheless re-execs FTL for a change
+# to ANY of its three files: `addn-hosts=` is itself a config directive, the
+# re-exec is already proven safe and costs ~1.1 s (measured: exactly one 50 ms
+# probe missed per re-exec, 1.06-1.13 s from that probe to the next successful
+# answer, over four measured re-execs), and one code path is one thing to get
+# wrong at 2am.
+#
+# Do NOT run `pihole restartdns` on this image: it hangs forever and can leave
+# the s6 service marked down, taking homelab DNS with it. Re-run the role.
+
+# --- wildcard subtree: forge.bi-services.be and every label beneath it ---
+# A for the apex and *.forge.bi-services.be
 address=/forge.bi-services.be/192.168.50.167
+# AAAA (and every other qtype) -> NOERROR / ANSWER: 0, served locally.
+# Deleting this line reintroduces the Windows "server IP address could not be
+# found" bug. It is NOT a synthetic IPv6 answer and NOT a global filter-AAAA.
+address=/forge.bi-services.be/

